# Lettering that belongs in a pencil drawing.
#
# Applies to whichever layer is active — meant for type, straight after it is
# drawn. Three things together make a letterform read as drawn rather than
# typeset, and none of them works alone: an outline, so the shape is a *line*;
# cross hatching inside it, which is what a pencil actually does to fill an
# area; and a faint wash of the fill underneath so the letters have some body.
#
# The wash is the layer's own pixels at low opacity. Everything else is an
# effect, and effects escape fill opacity — which is exactly what lets the
# outline and the hatching stay at full strength while the fill fades.
#
# `outline` and `hatch` are in pixels, so both are relative to the *type size*,
# not to the canvas. Outline scales with the type — roughly a sixtieth of the
# cap size, so 5 at 290-pixel type.
#
# Hatch does not, and it is worth knowing why. A hatch of 14 that suits type at
# 290 pixels also suits type at 170, where scaling it proportionally to 8 makes
# it vanish. The hatching stands for the width of a pencil, and a pencil is the
# width it is regardless of how large the letters are — so treat this one as an
# absolute mark size and only reach for it when the drawing itself is scaled.
#
# Judge these at 1:1 and not on a preview. Shrinking an image averages the
# hatching away and flatters it; at full size a hatch that is too coarse stops
# reading as graphite and starts reading as plaid.

param ink = #2e2e2e
param outline = 1.8
param hatch = 5
param texture = 0.5
param wash = 0.3

set fill-opacity={wash}
effect pattern-overlay pattern="Cross Hatch" color={ink} background=transparent scale={hatch} angle=32 opacity={texture}
effect stroke size={outline} color={ink} position=inside
